<article class="prose">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>Quick start</h1>
  <p class="lede">About fifteen minutes to a budget you can spend from. You add transactions, mark the ones that match your statement as cleared, then reconcile each account. ZxBudget has no account of its own. You can stop after any step and come back.</p>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>1. Open the app</h2>
      <p>Open ZxBudget on your iPhone, iPad, or Mac. The first screen is <strong>Welcome to ZxBudget</strong>. To use the same budget on your other Apple devices, sign into iCloud on each one (the iPhone Settings app, or System Settings on a Mac). One device works without that.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/welcome.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="Welcome to ZxBudget, with Create my budget and I'm joining a shared budget.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/welcome-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="Welcome to ZxBudget, with Create my budget and I'm joining a shared budget.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>2. Create your budget</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Choose <strong>Create my budget</strong>. If someone is inviting you instead, choose <strong>I'm joining a shared budget</strong> and accept their invite. Nothing is created on this device until the shared budget arrives.</li>
        <li>On <strong>Your Budget</strong>, set the name and the base currency. Canadian dollars is already selected. Tap <strong>Next</strong>.</li>
        <li>On <strong>How detailed?</strong>, pick a starting structure: Simple, Balanced, Detailed, or Blank. Balanced is the one selected to start. Tap <strong>Create</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>You can rename, add, or delete categories later. The template is only a starting point.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/details.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="Your Budget, with the name and base currency set to Canadian dollars.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/details-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="Your Budget, with the name and base currency set to Canadian dollars.">
      </figure>
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/template.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="How detailed, with Simple, Balanced, Detailed, and Blank. Balanced is selected.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/template-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="How detailed, with Simple, Balanced, Detailed, and Blank. Balanced is selected.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>3. Set your account balances</h2>
      <p>Open the <strong>Spending</strong> tab and tap <strong>All Accounts</strong>. A new budget includes <strong>Chequing</strong> and <strong>Credit Card</strong>, both at zero. Open Chequing. A new account asks for <strong>Balance</strong> and <strong>As of</strong>, which is the straightforward way to enter what the account holds today. Add savings, cash, or another card the same way.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/accounts.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Accounts list, with Chequing and Credit Card both at zero.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/accounts-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Accounts list, with Chequing and Credit Card both at zero.">
      </figure>
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/chequing.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Chequing account, starting at a working balance of zero.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/chequing-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Chequing account, starting at a working balance of zero.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>4. Record income</h2>
      <p>On iPhone, tap the <strong>+</strong> in the tab bar. On Mac, use <strong>Add Transaction</strong> or press ⌘N.</p>
      <p>Switch the amount to <strong>Inflow</strong>. The category starts at <strong>Ready to Assign</strong>. Enter the pay and the account it landed in, then save. That money waits in Ready to Assign until you give it a category.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/inflow.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="A new transaction set to Inflow, category Ready to Assign, account Chequing.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/inflow-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="A new transaction set to Inflow, category Ready to Assign, account Chequing.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>5. Assign the month</h2>
      <p>Open the <strong>Budget</strong> tab. What is still unassigned shows as <strong>To Budget</strong>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>On Mac, click a category’s <strong>Assigned</strong> amount and type what this month needs.</li>
        <li>On iPhone, tap the category, such as Groceries, and edit <strong>Assigned</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Stop when nothing is left to budget, or leave some of it there on purpose. Unassigned money stays visible.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/budget.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Budget tab, with To Budget at zero and categories such as Groceries under Essentials.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/budget-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Budget tab, with To Budget at zero and categories such as Groceries under Essentials.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>6. Record spending</h2>
      <p>Add another transaction and leave it on <strong>Outflow</strong>. Pick the category you spent from and save. That category’s available amount drops by what you spent.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/outflow.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="A new transaction set to Outflow. Choose a category before saving.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/outflow-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="A new transaction set to Outflow. Choose a category before saving.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>7. Mark transactions cleared</h2>
      <p>A new transaction is not cleared. At the end of its row is a <strong>C</strong>. An outline C has not been matched to your statement. A filled C has. A lock means that row was already reconciled, and it stays locked.</p>
      <p>When the purchase shows on your statement, mark it cleared. On iPhone, press and hold the row and tap <strong>Mark Cleared</strong>. On Mac, click the C. Uncleared rows stay out of the next step. Reconcile only locks the cleared ones.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/cleared.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The register in sample data. A filled C means cleared. A lock means that transaction is already reconciled.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/cleared-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The register in sample data. A filled C means cleared. A lock means that transaction is already reconciled.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>8. Reconcile your accounts</h2>
      <p>When the cleared transactions match your statement, open the account from <strong>Spending</strong>. Under <strong>Cleared</strong>, tap <strong>Reconcile</strong>.</p>
      <p>The sheet shows <strong>Statement balance</strong>, already filled with the cleared balance as of that date. Change the amount only when the statement differs, set <strong>As of</strong> to the statement date, and tap <strong>Reconcile</strong>. Cleared transactions on or before that date are locked. If the numbers differ, an adjustment is posted for that day.</p>
      <p>Do the same for each chequing account and credit card. Home lists <strong>Reconcile accounts</strong> when one has gone a while without it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/reconcile.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Reconcile sheet: statement balance, the as-of date, and the Reconcile button.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/reconcile-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="The Reconcile sheet: statement balance, the as-of date, and the Reconcile button.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
      <div class="note">
        <p>Want to click around before entering your own numbers? On a new budget, Home offers <strong>Explore with Sample Data</strong>. You can also open <strong>Try Demo Mode</strong> in Settings. On iPhone, Settings is in the <strong>···</strong> menu. On Mac, it is in the ZxBudget menu (⌘,). <strong>Exit Demo</strong> on the banner is the way back. The sample budget never mixes with yours.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img class="shot-light" src="/images/quick-start/sample.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="Home on a new budget, with Explore with Sample Data.">
        <img class="shot-dark" src="/images/quick-start/sample-dark.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="Home on a new budget, with Explore with Sample Data.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

